  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/...\nA sharp cooldown, precipitation, thunder, and winds are all in\nstore as successive troughs of low pressure move through the \narea. Wednesday through Friday, a closed low off the coast of \nCalifornia will rejoin the broader flow as a trough amplifies \nover the Pacific Northwest. A slight ridge axis over the region \nwill allow slight warming on Wednesday as clouds increase ahead \nof the approaching Californian low. Temperatures that afternoon \nwill be 10-15 degrees above normal. Wednesday evening, \nprecipitation chances begin to increase substantially to 50-70%.\nBroad cloud cover will inhibit instability, but the colder \natmospheric mid-layer with still warm surface temperatures allow\nfor a 10-20% chance of thunder Wednesday evening. Clouds, \nprecipitation, and a colder airmass moving in drop temperatures \nto near normal on Thursday, with precipitation chances still \n50-80% and just warm enough for a 10% chance of thunder that \nafternoon and evening. Before precipitation tapers off Friday \nevening, Friday morning the cold front passes, cooling temps to \nabout 5 degrees below normal and bringing wind gusts up to 25-35\nmph out of the NW. Winds remain strong through Friday evening. \nOver the course of the event, rainfall accumulation is forecast \nat 0.05-0.3 tenths in lower elevations, and 0.25-0.5 inches for \nmountain valleys. Mountain summits and ridges could see 0.5-1 \ninch of rainfall, and 1-4 inches of snow on summits above 8000 \nft MSL. Snow is unlikely on mountains until Friday morning.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to be near normal Saturday through\nSunday, with another significant upper-level trough looking to \nimpact the area Sunday. Behind the earlier system, snow levels \nare lower at 6500 ft MSL. Precipitation on Sunday will fall as \nsnow in mountain passes and the highest mountain towns if the \ntemperature forecast holds steady. Also behind the earlier \ntrough, mountain valley cold pools could support fog/stratus \nformation and allow snowfall in the valleys on Sunday.",
